 
          <p><code>cheese steak jimmy's</code> is next to <code>lumberjack</code> the first thing I&rsquo;d type in the chatbox after starting a random scenario in <em>Age of Empires II: The Age of Kings</em>, instantly granting me 10000 food and wood respectively. Why? Because it&rsquo;s fun to mess around in the game! I only now learned that the cheatcode is a reference to <a href="https://www.forgottenempires.net/strategy/age-of-empires-ii-strategy-center/cheat-codes">a restaurant near Ensemble Studios&rsquo; HQ</a> where the crew used to go for lunch a lot.</p>
<p>I have not played <em>Age of Empires</em> in ages, yet yesterday <code>jimmy's</code> spontaneously came to mind. It wasn&rsquo;t because I was hungry but because I was thinking about cheat codes in (PC) games. In <a href="/post/2025/06/the-pc-does-what-nintendont-modding-games/">The PC Does What Nintendon&rsquo;t: Modding Games</a> I summarised some of my favourite game mods. Is modding cheating? Is going out for a cheese steak cheating? Anyway, in this post I thought it would be fun to try and remember a few iconic cheat codes we relied on to have some fun without looking them up.</p>
<p>In the category of instant resource gain, I remember a few shooter specifics: <code>dnstuff</code> unlocks all of The Duke&rsquo;s weaponry in <em>Duke Nukem 3D</em>. Who doesn&rsquo;t like to start off with a fully loaded Rocked Propelled Generator to blast those aliens into bloody pieces? Using that cheat in the first level isn&rsquo;t even needed if that&rsquo;s what you&rsquo;re after: jump off the roof, go to the right, jump on the boxes and onto the ledge and an RPG will appear. Ta-daa! Are the aliens kicking your ass instead of the other way around? <code>dngod</code> will solve that in a pinch.</p>
<p>Speaking of retro shooters, the <em>DOOM</em> cheat codes are easy to remember if you know who made the game (iD Software): <code>iddqd</code> toggles invincibility on and off and <code>idkfa</code> grants all weapons, armour, and more importantly: all three keys. Some of the more labyrinthian levels make it particularly challenging to retrieve the last key making <code>idkfa</code> an attractive shortcut to the next level. There are more than enough games where I wish a cheat system to skip a level was included (<a href="https://jefklakscodex.com/games/pc/rayman-2/">Rayman 2</a> I recently replayed comes to mind: those race sequences are horrible but the rest of the game is just wonderful). I think there&rsquo;s also a cheatcode that toggles wall clipping negating the requirement of a key to enter a door. Ghost mode, yay!</p>
<p>What about the usage of &ldquo;trainers&rdquo;? A trainer is a small executable that alters the game state while it is running in order to grant the player clear advantages such as unlimited lives. Wall hacks aside, everybody used a <em>Diablo II</em> trainer back in the day to automatically collect gold&mdash;a feature that thankfully made it into the game with the <em>Resurrected</em> remaster. <em>Diablo II</em> was frequently plagued (and almost ruined) by the wrong kind of hacks &amp; trainers that allows players to quickly snag all the good stuff before you are able to click on them. Blizzard North kept on churning out patches to fix leaky holes and hackers kept on tugging and pulling to discover new ones. Needless to say, only use a trainer for fun in a singleplayer game: don&rsquo;t ruin the core game loop for others as well!</p>
<p>I also remember making use of a <em>Wizardry 8</em> trainer by the late Flamestryke that did not grant gold or HP but instead sped up the animations by a significant margin. Grinding in that game can be a real slog because in bigger battles it literally takes ages for the enemy to reposition because of these animations. Most of the warez rips that came with <a href="/post/2025/10/i-owe-warez-for-properly-discovering-crpgs/">Twilight CDs</a> included these little tools. When I was younger that was the only way for me to keep that HP bar from dropping to zero in the <em>Might &amp; Magic</em> series.</p>
<p>What about editing savegame files, either by hand or using a tool to streamline the process? <em>Baldur&rsquo;s Gate II</em> must have been the game where I fiddled with my savegames the most. Levelling up grants weapon proficiency pips depending on your (multi)class and I had a hard time specialising so I experimented by swapping these around in the editor. There was also a neat trick to pull off with a limitless Bag of Holding but I can&rsquo;t remember the specifics. Oh, and using these character editors, you can resurrect Yoshimo and keep him around as a permanent NPC. There isn&rsquo;t any scripted interaction with him after the betrayal scene though.</p>
<p>Most of my cheat experience stems from nineties and early noughties PC games. If you are part of the elite few that know the Konami code, I salute you: a surprising number of recent releases pay tribute to that code, unlocking goofy animations or extras. I did &ldquo;hack&rdquo; the Game Boy using <a href="/post/2023/08/an-ode-to-game-boy-cartridges/">a Game Genie</a> so I guess that counts as employing cheat codes as well. The Game Genie directly alters hardware register values, something you can <a href="/post/2022/04/password-hacking-sylvester-and-tweety/">experiment with yourself using an emulator</a>.</p>
<p>Which classic cheats do you still know by heart?</p>


          
